description:
In repos/goal-host-vessel/src/index.ts, stop pruneStore from deleting dispatch records after ~3 hours; compact the old ones instead so operator feedback can still grade them.

WHY. pruneStore deletes the 20 oldest records whenever the store passes 100, so /workspace/goal-host-dispatches.json holds about 99 records covering roughly 188 minutes. Operator verdicts are written with goal_verification_label_write and attach by execution_id, and the dispatch record is the only thing that carries executionId — so a dispatch older than about three hours cannot be graded at all. An operator reviewing a day of self-development finds most of it unlabelable, which makes "observe and give feedback" unusable as a posture. Measured on the live store: 99 records, 547KB, 5.5KB each, and 84 percent of those bytes are walk diagnostics — stateSignature 25.5 percent, steps 24.0, walkLog 14.2, poolProvenance 12.3, poolEvents 7.7. A record reduced to what a verdict needs is 319 bytes, so a full day at the observed rate of about 32 dispatches per hour costs roughly 240KB compacted versus 4.2MB uncompacted. That distinction matters because persistDispatchStore rewrites the whole file every 5 seconds, so keeping full records for a day would mean sustained megabyte-per-second writes.

THE CHANGE. Exactly one op. Find these four lines, which occur exactly once in the file:

  if (executionStore.size > 100) {
    const oldest = [...executionStore.entries()].sort((a, b) => a[1].startedAt - b[1].startedAt);
    for (const [id] of oldest.slice(0, 20)) executionStore.delete(id);
  }

Replace those four lines with exactly these lines:

  const byNewest = [...executionStore.entries()].sort((a, b) => b[1].startedAt - a[1].startedAt);
  for (const [, r] of byNewest.slice(100)) {
    const d = r as unknown as Record<string, unknown>;
    if (d["compacted"] === true) continue;
    d["stateSignature"] = undefined;
    d["steps"] = [];
    d["walkLog"] = [];
    d["poolProvenance"] = [];
    d["poolEvents"] = [];
    d["compacted"] = true;
  }
  for (const [id] of byNewest.slice(2000)) executionStore.delete(id);

WHAT THIS CHANGES AT RUNTIME. This is not cosmetic. Today a dispatch is DELETED once 100 newer ones exist, so its executionId is unrecoverable and no verdict can be attached. Afterwards the newest 100 keep full walk detail, records beyond that are stripped of the five heavy diagnostic fields but KEEP dispatchId, executionId, status, reached, selectedTemplateId, startedAt, endedAt, operator and goal, and deletion only happens beyond 2000 records — about two and a half days at the current rate. JSON.stringify omits properties whose value is undefined, so setting stateSignature to undefined removes it from the persisted file rather than writing null. The compacted flag makes the pass idempotent, so a record is stripped once rather than on every call.

SCOPE. One replacement only, inside pruneStore. Do not change persistDispatchStore, its 5 second setInterval, the DISPATCH_STORE_PATH constant, or the pruneStore call site. Compacted records will return empty walkLog and steps from goalWalkState, which is correct and intended: that detail also lives in the execution traces, whereas executionId exists nowhere else.

